{"verdict":"request_changes","spec_verification":[{"spec_quote":"@strawberry.field
def define_view(self, info: strawberry.types.Info, feature: Optional[str] = None) -> Optional[DefineView]:
    project_root = info.context["project_root"]
    return define.get_define_view(project_root, feature)","spec_section":"API Surface — Query field","satisfied":true,"evidence":"schema.py lines 552-557 match the spec signature exactly: accepts optional `feature: Optional[str] = None`, returns `Optional[DefineView]`, reads `project_root` from `info.context`, and delegates to `define.get_define_view(project_root, feature)`.","notes":"Clean match."},{"spec_quote":"When `feature` is provided, the resolver filters `DefineView.features` to only those whose name ends with the given string (same `str.endswith()` semantics as `spec_alignment`). Aggregates are recomputed from the filtered list. Defects are not filtered (they are project-wide, not per-feature).","spec_section":"API Surface — Query field","satisfied":true,"evidence":"define.py `get_define_view` (lines 673–751): filters with `f.name.endswith(feature)` after assembling all features, recomputes aggregates via `_compute_aggregates(features)` on the filtered list, and builds `defects` outside the filter block.","notes":"All three sub-requirements met."},{"spec_quote":"`_read_guardian_verdict()` | Guardian logs (JSON, JSONL, cache) | `DefineFeature.built.guardianVerdict`","spec_section":"Existing Infrastructure — Backend readers","satisfied":false,"evidence":"define.py defines its own `_read_guardian_verdict` (lines 379–421) instead of importing and calling `_read_guardian_verdict` from `landing.py`. The re-implementation omits the `cache/guardian.dat` fallback that landing.py checks as its third source (exit code mapping: 0=approve, 1=reject, 2=flag, 3=error). The return type also diverges: landing.py returns `str` (defaults to `"unknown"`), define.py returns `str | None` (defaults to `None`). Guardian verdicts stored only in the cache will be silently lost.","notes":"Behavioral regression. The spec is explicit that this function is reusable — the table says so directly. Rebuilding it instead of importing it creates a silent divergence."},{"spec_quote":"`_read_draft_specs()` | `specs/product/*.md`, matches tech/design specs, reads feature state | `DefineFeature.specified` (spec existence, feature state)","spec_section":"Existing Infrastructure — Backend readers","satisfied":"partial","evidence":"define.py re-implements spec matching from scratch via `_match_specs_to_feature()` and `_build_specified()` rather than building on `_read_draft_specs`. The existing function already globs product/tech/design specs, matches by filename, and checks feature state — exactly what the define resolver needs. The difference is `_read_draft_specs` filters out executing/done features for the landing panel, which `get_define_view` should not do. That difference is real and valid. But the glob-and-match logic is duplicated wholesale rather than extracted.","notes":"The spec says 'reuse these rather than rebuilding from scratch.' The filtering difference is a legitimate reason not to call `_read_draft_specs` directly, but the spec-existence and spec-matching logic (globs, companion spec detection, frontmatter fallback) should have been shared helpers, not duplicated."},{"spec_quote":"`_read_defects()` | `.speed/defects/*/state.json` + `specs/defects/*.md` | `DefineView.defects` (needs enrichment: add description, impact, filedAt)","spec_section":"Existing Infrastructure — Backend readers","satisfied":"partial","evidence":"define.py implements `_build_defects()` + `_read_defect_description()` + `_parse_severity_from_md()` from scratch. The spec says to reuse `_read_defects()` from landing.py and enrich it, not rebuild it. The enrichment (description, impact, filedAt) is correctly added, but the base read logic is duplicated.","notes":"Lower severity than `_read_guardian_verdict` because there is no behavioral divergence — the same file sources are read. But still violates the explicit reuse instruction."},{"spec_quote":"Open questions: Count of rows in the Open Questions table where Status is 'Open'","spec_section":"Data Model — Audit computation","satisfied":false,"evidence":"define.py `_build_specified` counts open questions as lines starting with `"?"` or containing `"open question"` (case-insensitive). The spec says to count table rows where the Status column is `"Open"`. These are different heuristics: a spec file with an `## Open Questions` table and `| Q1 | Open |` rows would parse correctly with the spec approach but inconsistently with the define.py approach.","notes":"The heuristic will over-count (any `?`-prefixed line in the spec body triggers a hit) or under-count (a properly formatted table row with `Open` status but no `?` on the row does not). Not a crash risk but produces incorrect data."},{"spec_quote":"`DefineFeature.state` | One of `"complete"`, `"executing"`, `"writing"`, `"unplanned"`. Derived from `state.json`. Features without a `state.json` default to `"unplanned"`.","spec_section":"Validation Rules","satisfied":true,"evidence":"`_read_feature_state()` (lines 650–656) reads state.json and defaults to `"unplanned"` if absent. `get_define_view` calls it per feature. The four state values are used in `_compute_aggregates` (lines 607–644) which counts `completed_count`, `executing_count`, and `unplanned_count`.","notes":"State derivation and default match the spec."},{"spec_quote":"`DefineDefect.severity` | One of `"P0"`, `"P1"`, `"P2"`, `"P3"`. Unknown severities clamped to `"P3"`.","spec_section":"Validation Rules","satisfied":true,"evidence":"`_build_defects()` clamps unknown severity to `"P3"` via `_VALID_SEVERITIES` check. Defects are sorted by `_SEVERITY_ORDER` (P0 first).","notes":"Matches spec."},{"spec_quote":"`BuiltData.coveragePct` | Float 0.0-100.0 or null. Null for features that have not been executed. Clamped to [0, 100].","spec_section":"Validation Rules","satisfied":true,"evidence":"`_build_built()` at the traceability read: `coverage_pct = max(0.0, min(100.0, pct * 100 if pct <= 1.0 else pct))`. Handles both ratio (0–1) and percentage (0–100) input formats. Returns `None` when file is absent.","notes":"Correct."},{"spec_quote":"Spec-to-feature matching: filename convention... If the filename doesn't match, the resolver falls back to scanning spec frontmatter for a `feature:` field.","spec_section":"Data Model — Spec-to-feature matching","satisfied":true,"evidence":"`_match_specs_to_feature()` checks `spec_dir / f"{feature_name}.md"` first, then globs all `*.md` in the spec dir and reads `feature:` from frontmatter via `_read_frontmatter_feature()`.","notes":"Both match strategies implemented correctly."},{"spec_quote":"1. **Structural checks**: Required sections present (Problem, Users, User Stories, Scope, Success Criteria for PRD; Basic Example, Data Model, API Surface for RFC)","spec_section":"Data Model — Audit computation","satisfied":true,"evidence":"`_PRD_REQUIRED_SECTIONS` and `_RFC_REQUIRED_SECTIONS` constants match the spec exactly. `_audit_spec()` uses case-insensitive substring matching on headings extracted by `_extract_headings()`.","notes":"Correct sections, correct match strategy."},{"spec_quote":"`SpecIndicator.auditStatus` | One of `"clean"`, `"warning"`, `"error"`.","spec_section":"Validation Rules","satisfied":"partial","evidence":"`_audit_spec()` only produces `"clean"` or `"error"`. `"warning"` is never returned. The spec's Validation Rules say warning is for optional sections missing, and V1 defines no optional sections (only required sections for PRD/RFC), so `"warning"` can never trigger in V1. Acceptable for V1 scope.","notes":"Fine for V1, but the `SpecIndicator` type contract advertises three states. Future additions of optional sections will require `_audit_spec` changes."},{"spec_quote":"`DefineAggregates` | All fields are non-negative integers. Computed from the `features` array, not independently queried.","spec_section":"Validation Rules","satisfied":true,"evidence":"`_compute_aggregates(features)` takes the filtered feature list and derives all counts in-memory. No separate file reads.","notes":"Correct."}],"missing_from_spec":[{"spec_quote":"Sizing estimate: Parsed from the audit output or estimated from user story count (rough heuristic: stories * 2 tasks)","description":"define.py computes sizing estimate by scanning the product spec for user story lines under a 'User Stor*' heading and multiplying by 2. This matches the fallback heuristic. However, the spec says it should *first* be parsed from the audit output, with the story-count heuristic as a fallback. There is no audit output artifact in V1 ('V1 audit is computed at query time, no cached artifact'), so the story-count path is the only valid path — but the ordering in the spec implies a primary source exists. This is acceptable for V1 but worth noting."}],"out_of_scope":[{"file":"dashboard/backend/resolvers/define.py","line":54,"description":"`_read_json` and `_read_json_lenient` are two nearly-identical functions differing only in return type annotation (`dict[str, Any] | None` vs `Any`). Their bodies are identical. This is minor internal duplication with no spec requirement for two separate functions. One parametric reader would suffice."},{"file":"dashboard/backend/resolvers/define.py","line":43,"description":"`_EXECUTING_STATES` and `_COMPLETED_STATES` frozensets are defined but do not appear to be used by any function in the file. Feature state mapping in `get_define_view` reads the raw state string from state.json and passes it through — there is no normalization step that uses these constants. Dead code."}],"issues":[{"severity":"major","file":"dashboard/backend/resolvers/define.py","line":379,"message":"`_read_guardian_verdict` re-implements logic that already exists in `landing.py` and omits the `cache/guardian.dat` fallback. landing.py's version checks three sources in order: guardian-*.json, Guardian-*.jsonl, then cache/guardian.dat with exit code mapping (0=approve, 1=reject, 2=flag, 3=error). define.py's version only checks the first two. Any project that stores Guardian verdicts in the cache file will return `None` from the define resolver while returning a verdict from the landing resolver — silent behavioral divergence between two pages showing the same data.","suggestion":"Import and call `_read_guardian_verdict` from `landing.py` instead. The only adaptation needed is handling the return type: landing.py returns `str` (never None, defaults to `"unknown"`), so callers should treat `"unknown"` as the null sentinel rather than checking for None. Alternatively, change the landing.py function to return `Optional[str]` (returning None instead of `"unknown"`) and import that."},{"severity":"major","file":"dashboard/backend/resolvers/define.py","line":265,"message":"Open questions counting reads lines starting with `"?"` or containing `"open question"` (case-insensitive). The spec says to count 'rows in the Open Questions table where Status is "Open"'. A spec author who writes a properly structured Markdown table with a Status column will get zero open questions counted here (table cells don't start with `?`). A spec that uses prose questions like `? What is the retention policy?` will be counted even if it has no Open Questions table. The heuristic is inconsistent with the spec's defined source.","suggestion":"Parse the Open Questions table: find a heading matching `/open questions?/i`, then read the following table rows and count those whose Status cell is `"Open"` (case-insensitive). Fall back to zero if no such table exists."},{"severity":"minor","file":"dashboard/backend/resolvers/define.py","line":54,"message":"`_read_json_lenient` is identical to `_read_json` except for its return annotation (`Any` vs `dict[str, Any] | None`). Two functions for the same operation adds maintenance surface with no benefit.","suggestion":"Remove `_read_json_lenient`. Replace all callsites with `_read_json` and use `isinstance` checks at the callsite where a non-dict type is expected (e.g. `cv_data = _read_json(cv_file)` then `if isinstance(cv_data, list): ...`)."},{"severity":"minor","file":"dashboard/backend/resolvers/define.py","line":43,"message":"`_EXECUTING_STATES` and `_COMPLETED_STATES` are defined but unused. Unreferenced constants in a new file suggest either leftover scaffold or a state normalization step that was intended but not implemented.","suggestion":"Remove them, or use them in `get_define_view` to normalize the raw state string from `state.json` to one of the four spec-defined values (`complete`, `executing`, `writing`, `unplanned`). Currently `get_define_view` passes the raw state.json value through without validation."},{"severity":"nit","file":"dashboard/backend/resolvers/define.py","line":673,"message":"`get_define_view` discovers features from two sources: `.speed/features/` directories and `specs/product/*.md` files that lack a corresponding `.speed/features/` entry. The second source (orphan product specs) produces features with state `"unplanned"`. This is reasonable behavior but is not described in the spec — the spec only mentions `.speed/features/` as the source of feature names. Worth confirming this is intended.","suggestion":"If intentional, add a brief docstring comment explaining why orphan product specs are surfaced. If not intentional, remove the second discovery loop."}],"strengths":["Schema registration in schema.py is an exact match to the spec's defined query field signature — return type, parameter name, parameter default, context extraction, and delegate call are all correct.","Feature filter uses `str.endswith` semantics as the spec requires, aggregates are recomputed post-filter, and defects are intentionally excluded from the filter. These three sub-requirements are all present.","Severity clamping to `"P3"` for unknown severity values is implemented and defects are sorted by severity order (P0 first), matching the spec's validation rules.","Spec-to-feature matching correctly implements both the filename convention (primary) and frontmatter `feature:` field fallback, which the spec calls out as an unresolved question that was solved.","coverage_pct handles both ratio-format (0–1) and percentage-format (0–100) inputs from spec-traceability.json, clamped to [0, 100]. Defensive and correct.","Frontmatter quote stripping in `_read_frontmatter_feature` handles both single and double quoted values."]}
